<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?> <?xml-stylesheet type="text/xsl" href="rss.xsl"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"> <channel> <title>Thor Henning Hetland</title><description>Software architect, AI development pioneer, founder of JavaZone and eXOReaction</description><link>https://wiki.totto.org/</link><atom:link href="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml" /> <managingEditor>Thor Henning Hetland</managingEditor><language>en</language> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 18:49:16 -0000</pubDate> <lastBuildDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 18:49:16 -0000</lastBuildDate> <ttl>1440</ttl> <generator>MkDocs RSS plugin - v1.19.0</generator> <image> <url>None</url> <title>Thor Henning Hetland</title> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/</link> </image> <item> <title>The Zombie in the Basement</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <author>Claude Code</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>Architecture</category> <category>ai-agents</category> <category>architecture</category> <category>documentation</category> <category>graph</category> <category>migrations</category> <category>verification</category> <description>Static architecture catalogs document what someone knew at a point in time. Betonix GmbH thought they had 23 database connections. They had 52. The migration nearly shipped that delta.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/19/the-zombie-in-the-basement/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 18:47:56 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2026/06/19/the-zombie-in-the-basement/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Cloud Computing: Are You Looking for IaaS or PaaS Provider?</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>amazon</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>google</category> <category>iaas</category> <category>microsoft</category> <category>paas</category> <description>Comparing IaaS and PaaS cloud models from Amazon, Google, and Microsoft -- and why the choice between platform and infrastructure shapes everything.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/cloud-computing-are-you-looking-for-iaas-or-paas-provider/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/cloud-computing-are-you-looking-for-iaas-or-paas-provider/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Clouded Vision</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>architecture</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>enterprise</category> <description>Cloud savings come from zero-admin architecture, not cheaper servers. Most apps carry enterprise DNA that makes migration painful without rethinking the design.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/clouded-vision/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/clouded-vision/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Open Source and Community Clouds</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>eucalyptus</category> <category>open-source</category> <category>stratuslab</category> <description>Early open-source cloud platforms StratusLab and Eucalyptus offered EC2-compatible infrastructure on your own hardware, with real management challenges.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/open-source-and-community-clouds/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/open-source-and-community-clouds/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Sinnataggen and some Cloud development thoughts...</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>development</category> <category>gartner</category> <category>platforms</category> <description>Gartner&#39;s six criteria for evaluating cloud development platforms, from interoperability and mobile support to vendor longevity and ecosystem quality.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/sinnataggen-and-some-cloud-development-thoughts/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/sinnataggen-and-some-cloud-development-thoughts/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Trouble In The Clouds - Gmail Turns Into Gfail</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>gmail</category> <category>google</category> <category>reliability</category> <category>saas</category> <description>Gmail&#39;s February 2009 outage exposed cloud reliability gaps and Google&#39;s slow communication -- three hours before acknowledging a widespread service failure.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/trouble-in-the-clouds---gmail-turns-into-gfail/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/trouble-in-the-clouds---gmail-turns-into-gfail/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Why clouds should be more like operating systems</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>amazon</category> <category>aws</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>ec2</category> <category>sysadmin</category> <description>Managing multiple AMIs still requires manual sysadmin. Clouds need dashboard tooling and automated administration to deliver on their promise.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/why-clouds-should-be-more-like-operating-systems/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/02/24/why-clouds-should-be-more-like-operating-systems/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Its time for auto scaling - avoid peak load provisioning for web applications</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>auto-scaling</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>middleware</category> <category>virtualization</category> <description>Applications cannot use new compute resources dynamically without middleware virtualisation. Auto-scaling requires architecture, not just infrastructure.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/03/05/its-time-for-auto-scaling---avoid-peak-load-provisioning-for-web-applications/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/03/05/its-time-for-auto-scaling---avoid-peak-load-provisioning-for-web-applications/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Cloud - auto-magic or just provisioning platform for your distributed system</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>distributed-systems</category> <category>enterprise</category> <category>provisioning</category> <description>Clouds are provisioning platforms, not silver bullets. Enterprises still want magic, but the real question is whether to educate or go with the flow.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/05/07/cloud---auto-magic-or-just-provisioning-platform-for-your-distributed-system/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/05/07/cloud---auto-magic-or-just-provisioning-platform-for-your-distributed-system/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Gartner on Cloud Computing, misses again...</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>gartner</category> <category>iaas</category> <category>paas</category> <category>saas</category> <description>Gartner&#39;s five cloud attributes conflate delivery models, deployment models, and key characteristics into a flat taxonomy that obscures more than it reveals.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/06/23/gartner-on-cloud-computing-misses-again/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/06/23/gartner-on-cloud-computing-misses-again/</guid> </item> <item> <title>On the greenness of Clouds</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>Cloud Computing</category> <category>cloud</category> <category>green-it</category> <category>sustainability</category> <category>virtualization</category> <description>Cloud computing is greener than in-house servers today, but componentisation drives consumption. If computation gets too cheap to measure, waste will follow.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/09/15/on-the-greenness-of-clouds/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2009/09/15/on-the-greenness-of-clouds/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Frøya: A Digital Co-Worker</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>ai-agent</category> <category>catalystone</category> <category>digital-co-worker</category> <category>froya</category> <category>human-ai-collaboration</category> <category>quadim</category> <category>skills</category> <description>Quadim&#39;s AI agent Froya maintains a public skill library at scale -- not replacing a human editor, but making continuous QA possible where none existed before.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/04/28/fr%C3%B8ya-a-digital-co-worker/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/04/28/fr%C3%B8ya-a-digital-co-worker/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Mapping Human Potential</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>ai-agent</category> <category>catalystone</category> <category>digital-co-worker</category> <category>dynamic-taxonomies</category> <category>froya</category> <category>human-ai-collaboration</category> <category>quadim</category> <category>rag</category> <category>skills</category> <description>Static skill taxonomies fail because skills are contextual. Quadim uses RAG, MCP, and dynamic taxonomies to build maps that adapt to their users.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/05/20/mapping-human-potential/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/05/20/mapping-human-potential/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Rethinking Systems for AI</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>ai-architecture</category> <category>aurora</category> <category>data-infrastructure</category> <category>systems-thinking</category> <category>temporal-analytics</category> <category>xorcery-aaa</category> <description>AI asks why, not what. Systems designed for current-state queries need three shifts -- events over state, graphs over tables, conversations over queries.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/08/25/rethinking-systems-for-ai/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/08/25/rethinking-systems-for-ai/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Aurora: Answering Why</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>aurora</category> <category>graph-database</category> <category>quadim</category> <category>root-cause-analysis</category> <category>temporal-analytics</category> <category>why-not-what</category> <category>xorcery-aaa</category> <description>Aurora combines a temporal graph database with an AI query layer to answer why questions -- pattern detection, forensic investigation, and ripple effects.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/09/01/aurora-answering-why/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/09/01/aurora-answering-why/</guid> </item> <item> <title>Unlocking Temporal Graphs</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>aurora</category> <category>bitemporal</category> <category>graph-database</category> <category>perfect-memory</category> <category>temporal-analytics</category> <category>xorcery-aaa</category> <description>A bitemporal model with four time dimensions -- transaction, valid, decision, and query -- enables perfect past recall and repeatable historical reporting.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/09/18/unlocking-temporal-graphs/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/09/18/unlocking-temporal-graphs/</guid> </item> <item> <title>From Data to Action: The Alchemy and Aurora Stack</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>alchemy</category> <category>aurora</category> <category>data-architecture</category> <category>reactive-systems</category> <category>temporal-analytics</category> <category>xorcery-aaa</category> <description>Alchemy handles reactive data ingestion via YAML config; Aurora stores temporal graph events. Two layers, independently scalable, cleanly separated.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/09/24/from-data-to-action-the-alchemy-and-aurora-stack/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/09/24/from-data-to-action-the-alchemy-and-aurora-stack/</guid> </item> <item> <title>The Organisational Amnesia Problem</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>aurora</category> <category>compliance</category> <category>data-architecture</category> <category>root-cause-analysis</category> <category>temporal-analytics</category> <category>xorcery-aaa</category> <description>Most systems overwrite history, making root cause analysis painfully manual. Temporal graphs make those questions answerable in seconds across six domains.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/10/10/the-organisational-amnesia-problem/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/10/10/the-organisational-amnesia-problem/</guid> </item> <item> <title>The Blind Spot of Now</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>amnesia</category> <category>aurora</category> <category>data-infrastructure</category> <category>temporal-analytics</category> <category>time-series</category> <category>xorcery-aaa</category> <description>Current-state databases delete causation. Two case studies show how temporal analytics shifts operations from reactive to predictive.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/10/28/the-blind-spot-of-now/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/10/28/the-blind-spot-of-now/</guid> </item> <item> <title>The LLM Cautionary Tales</title> <author>Thor Henning Hetland</author> <category>AI-Augmented Development</category> <category>agentic-ai</category> <category>ai-security</category> <category>cautionary-tales</category> <category>horror</category> <category>llm</category> <category>prompt-injection</category> <category>security</category> <description>Seven dramatised LLM failure scenarios -- runaway loops, prompt injection, data leaks, lying evaluators -- written as horror stories so the lessons stick.</description> <link>https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/11/05/the-llm-cautionary-tales/</link> <pubDate>Fri, 19 Jun 2026 17:13:29 +0000</pubDate> <source url="https://wiki.totto.org/feed_rss_updated.xml">Thor Henning Hetland</source><guid isPermaLink="true">https://wiki.totto.org/blog/2025/11/05/the-llm-cautionary-tales/</guid> </item> </channel> </rss>